WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:02.989
In the first lesson, you chose a work example

2
00:00:03.011 --> 00:00:05.833
and described what someone needs to do with it.

3
00:00:06.411 --> 00:00:08.656
Now we will examine what might get in the

4
00:00:08.678 --> 00:00:12.789
way. This lesson connects the principal standard with ongoing

5
00:00:12.811 --> 00:00:16.011
workforce learning and understanding community needs.

6
00:00:16.788 --> 00:00:20.054
Start with an observation you could describe without assigning

7
00:00:20.077 --> 00:00:23.854
a motive. For our sample notice, the observation is

8
00:00:23.877 --> 00:00:26.988
that some people have not responded. Saying that they

9
00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:30.299
do not care is an interpretation. Saying that they

10
00:00:30.321 --> 00:00:33.943
cannot understand the notice is also an interpretation until

11
00:00:33.966 --> 00:00:35.010
we have more information.

12
00:00:35.897 --> 00:00:40.008
A respectful inquiry begins by acknowledging what we do

13
00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:43.430
not know. We can say, where our role permits

14
00:00:43.452 --> 00:00:46.297
contact, that we want to check whether the person

15
00:00:46.319 --> 00:00:49.786
received the information and whether another way of explaining

16
00:00:49.808 --> 00:00:53.074
or responding would help. We do not need to

17
00:00:53.097 --> 00:00:56.074
begin by asking the person to defend their behavior.

18
00:00:57.321 --> 00:01:01.299
Imagine the steps involved in responding. The person must

19
00:01:01.321 --> 00:01:05.721
receive the notice, recognize its purpose, understand the options,

20
00:01:06.054 --> 00:01:08.210
decide what they want to do, and use an

21
00:01:08.232 --> 00:01:12.254
available response method. A difficulty at any one step

22
00:01:12.277 --> 00:01:14.299
can look like a lack of interest from the

23
00:01:14.321 --> 00:01:14.943
other end.

24
00:01:15.523 --> 00:01:20.546
Possible barriers include unfamiliar terminology, information in a format

25
00:01:20.723 --> 00:01:24.212
someone cannot access, no interpreter when needed, an inconvenient

26
00:01:24.234 --> 00:01:29.257
appointment, transportation, unreliable technology, or previous experiences that make

27
00:01:29.279 --> 00:01:32.723
the process difficult to trust. These are possibilities to

28
00:01:32.746 --> 00:01:35.990
investigate, not explanations to assign automatically.

29
00:01:36.734 --> 00:01:40.112
Also look for strengths and supports. A person may

30
00:01:40.134 --> 00:01:43.868
already have an effective communication method, a trusted support

31
00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.712
person they choose, or a clear preference about how

32
00:01:46.734 --> 00:01:50.379
to receive information. A community may have organizations with

33
00:01:50.401 --> 00:01:54.734
relevant knowledge and established relationships. Understanding assets as well

34
00:01:54.757 --> 00:01:57.557
as barriers is part of the community assessment described

35
00:01:57.579 --> 00:01:58.579
in standard twelve.

36
00:01:59.723 --> 00:02:04.368
Culture can shape communication, expectations and experiences. It does

37
00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:07.723
not determine every person's preferences. People within the same

38
00:02:07.746 --> 00:02:11.212
community differ, and a person's preferences can change with

39
00:02:11.234 --> 00:02:11.812
the situation.

40
00:02:12.668 --> 00:02:16.446
Avoid turning general information about a community into a

41
00:02:16.468 --> 00:02:19.868
rule about an individual. Instead of deciding that a

42
00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:23.223
family will want a particular person to speak, ask

43
00:02:23.246 --> 00:02:25.712
whom the person would like involved and how they

44
00:02:25.734 --> 00:02:29.757
want to participate. Instead of interpreting silence as agreement,

45
00:02:30.201 --> 00:02:32.446
offer time and a way to ask questions.

46
00:02:33.256 --> 00:02:36.256
Instead of treating an accent as evidence of understanding

47
00:02:36.278 --> 00:02:40.189
or misunderstanding, check the clarity of the information itself.

48
00:02:40.956 --> 00:02:45.022
Cultural humility involves examining your own assumptions as well.

49
00:02:45.622 --> 00:02:48.133
Familiar procedures can feel straightforward to the people who

50
00:02:48.156 --> 00:02:51.133
use them every day. They may be entirely unfamiliar

51
00:02:51.156 --> 00:02:54.444
to someone encountering them for the first time. Recognizing

52
00:02:54.467 --> 00:02:57.511
that difference is an opportunity to improve the explanation.

53
00:02:58.789 --> 00:03:02.944
Consider these two openings. The first asks, why have

54
00:03:02.967 --> 00:03:06.033
you failed to return the form? The second explains

55
00:03:06.056 --> 00:03:08.478
the purpose of the form, asks whether the person

56
00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:11.900
received it, and offers help identifying a usable way

57
00:03:11.922 --> 00:03:15.900
to respond. The second opening provides information and creates

58
00:03:15.922 --> 00:03:17.500
room for the person's explanation.

59
00:03:18.341 --> 00:03:21.541
That does not mean ignoring a deadline or a

60
00:03:21.563 --> 00:03:25.697
required step. It means explaining the requirement clearly while

61
00:03:25.719 --> 00:03:28.919
asking what support is needed. Do not promise an

62
00:03:28.941 --> 00:03:32.519
extension or exception that you cannot authorize. If an

63
00:03:32.541 --> 00:03:35.630
adjustment needs approval, explain what you can do next

64
00:03:35.652 --> 00:03:36.948
and who will review it.

65
00:03:37.448 --> 00:03:40.603
In the practice activity, separate what was observed from

66
00:03:40.626 --> 00:03:44.114
what was assumed. Then choose a question that invites

67
00:03:44.137 --> 00:03:48.114
the person's perspective without requiring private information that is

68
00:03:48.137 --> 00:03:49.470
not needed for the task.

69
00:03:50.552 --> 00:03:53.463
You may not have direct contact with people receiving

70
00:03:53.486 --> 00:03:57.486
services. If you work on policy, data, contracts or

71
00:03:57.508 --> 00:04:01.552
communications, inquiry still matters. Ask how the process was

72
00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:05.463
tested, whose experience informed it, which access needs were

73
00:04:05.486 --> 00:04:09.263
considered, and where feedback is recorded. Consult the appropriate

74
00:04:09.286 --> 00:04:12.708
people rather than inventing an answer or contacting someone

75
00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:14.086
outside your responsibilities.

76
00:04:14.874 --> 00:04:18.674
A supervisor can help staff discuss uncertainty without shaming

77
00:04:18.697 --> 00:04:21.497
them for asking questions. A manager can look for

78
00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:25.163
repeated barriers across encounters. A director can ask whether

79
00:04:25.186 --> 00:04:29.808
resources and accountability support the response. Regular learning, addressed

80
00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:33.008
in standard four, should help people practice these decisions

81
00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:35.674
rather than merely acknowledge that they read a policy.

82
00:04:36.886 --> 00:04:41.130
Return to your selected work example. Write one observation,

83
00:04:41.463 --> 00:04:44.019
one assumption you need to set aside, and one

84
00:04:44.041 --> 00:04:47.263
respectful question that would help you learn more. Then

85
00:04:47.286 --> 00:04:50.530
identify an appropriate way to obtain that information within

86
00:04:50.552 --> 00:04:51.108
your role.

87
00:04:51.662 --> 00:04:53.996
If there is no direct access to the people

88
00:04:54.018 --> 00:04:57.440
affected, do not fill the gap by guessing. Name

89
00:04:57.462 --> 00:05:01.062
the gap and identify who can help. Existing feedback,

90
00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:05.440
approved engagement processes, accessibility reviews or a discussion with

91
00:05:05.462 --> 00:05:08.462
the responsible program area may provide a starting point.

92
00:05:09.024 --> 00:05:12.824
The next lesson follows a language-assistance request through an

93
00:05:12.847 --> 00:05:15.691
encounter. Keep the habit from this lesson with you:

94
00:05:15.713 --> 00:05:19.669
ask about needs and preferences, describe what you know,

95
00:05:19.936 --> 00:05:22.291
and leave room for information that could change your

96
00:05:22.313 --> 00:05:23.180
first interpretation.
